<spell-notebook "value usage">
	<header level="1">
		Value
		<header>A value is what a statistic shows:  a number, a word, an icon or an image.</>
	</>
	<value-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#owners">Owners</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-value>` is what a statistic shows: a number, a word, an icon or an image, with a `<ui-label>` naming it. In a
			search result it is the price.

			- `text` makes a word value: smaller and bold, so "Three Thousand" fits.
			- Put a `<ui-icon>` or `<ui-image>` inside it beside the number.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Owners">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-value>` finds its nearest owner and sets `:state(in-<owner>)` on itself; `UIParts.css` styles it by that
			state. Its owners, and what each makes of it:

			- [Statistic](/components/ui/ui-statistic.html) (`:state(in-statistic)`): large and light, above the label;  the statistic's `size` scales it
			- [Search](/components/ui/ui-search.html) (`:state(in-search)`): a search result's price, on the right

			Anywhere else it is an unstyled block. More on how parts find their owners: [Content parts](/components/ui/ui-header.html).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- The value and its label are read in order ("40,509 Views"); keep them next to each other in the markup.
			- An icon inside a value is decorative: the number and the label carry the meaning.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
